// THE CATALOGUE. One entry per generated asset, for every game on the floor.
//
// This file is the source; the bytes under assets/out/ are the build output.
// Nothing here is generated by hand and nothing under out/ is edited by hand —
// change a prompt, run `node scripts/assets.mjs gen`, and the new art lands at
// a new filename because the filename carries the prompt's hash.
//
// It is plain ES modules with no imports, deliberately: node reads it in the
// CLI and the gate, and a browser could import it directly if it ever needed
// to. (It does not — the runtime reads the generated assets/index.json, the
// same way the arcade reads hub/versions.json rather than every VERSIONS.md.)
//
// ── WHAT BELONGS IN HERE, AND WHAT DOES NOT ────────────────────────────────
// This repo draws its art in code on purpose, and several of those systems are
// deliberate, finished, and locked: the hub's 128×72 covers, gameoflife's
// 16-colour polygon buffer, flashprince's rotoscope, Toko's brand mark. A
// generated PNG must not walk into any of them. It is not a rule about file
// types, it is that those systems say something a diffusion model cannot.
//
// So the first batch is the three places a generated asset is additive:
//
//   reference/  — plates the OWNER'S METHOD needs. CLAUDE.md's diagnosis is
//                 that every game got one prototype burst and no second one,
//                 and the way out is "a reference, then render → LOOK → name
//                 what is wrong → redo". That loop starts with a reference,
//                 and until now every reference had to come from the owner by
//                 hand. These guide code. They ship to nobody.
//   texture/    — the maps three.js genuinely wants and currently fakes with
//                 a CanvasTexture (skies, grounds, gradients). A drop-in.
//   prop/       — set dressing that does not exist yet, and so cannot be
//                 regressed by adding it. These are the 2D→3D chain: nano
//                 banana draws the plate, meshy lifts it into a mesh.
//
// Adding an asset is one entry here. Adding a GAME to the pipeline is one
// entry in STYLES.

// ── THE HOUSE REGISTER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Prepended to every prompt that names it, so "everything this workshop makes
// looks related" is one edit rather than forty. A spec's own words are
// appended after this block, closest to the model's attention.
export const STYLES = {
  // Toko Drop: gel. The material language is lifted straight out of
  // js/tuning.js — subsurface 0.70, roughness 0.16, clearcoat 1.0, a little
  // transmission. That is a wet satin sweet, and the prompts should say so in
  // words a model knows rather than in shader parameters it does not.
  gel: `Toko Drop house style. Translucent coloured gel and hard candy-glass.
Soft subsurface scattering, a wet clearcoat highlight, faint internal glow.
Rounded squat forms with weight, sitting on the ground rather than floating.
Clean studio lighting on a plain dark backdrop, no text, no logos, no UI.`,

  // Hyper Dagger: black and white with dark red as the ONLY contrast colour.
  // Stated that way in CLAUDE.md and it is load-bearing — a second hue in a
  // reference plate is what starts the drift.
  dagger: `Hyper Dagger house style. Black and white ONLY, with dark red as the
single contrast colour — no other hue anywhere in frame. Harsh neon-on-void
contrast, blown-out white highlights, deep crushed blacks. Bone, chrome and
ember. Menacing, weightless, synthwave void. No text, no logos, no UI.`,

  // Paper Route: flat and unlit on purpose — MeshBasicMaterial, no lights, no
  // shadows. A reference plate with soft shading would send the code the wrong
  // way, so the prompt has to forbid the thing a model most wants to do.
  dawn: `Paper Route house style. FLAT unlit vector illustration — no shading,
no gradients, no shadows, no ambient occlusion. Solid blocks of colour with
hard edges, like a screen-printed poster. Bright sunny-day palette: sky blue,
grey road, green lawn, mint and coral houses. Isometric 3/4 view. No text.`,

  // Neon Ronin: read off hub/art.js's `slash` cover and the catalogue accent
  // #e83ca8 — a violet-to-hot-magenta sky, cyan as the only cold colour in it,
  // and a lit violet cloak. The cover's own hard-won rule is in here too: a
  // hero must not be a black silhouette against a dark scene. That was learned
  // by drawing it wrong at 128×72, and it is just as true at 1024.
  neon: `Neon Ronin house style. Night city and temple neon: deep violet sky
ramping to hot magenta at the horizon, hot pink #e83ca8 as the key light,
electric cyan #2ae8e0 as the only cold accent. The figure is LIT, never a black
silhouette — pale violet cloak catching the light, with a magenta rim down one
edge and a cyan rim down the other. Sharp graphic shapes, high contrast,
16-bit box-art energy. No text, no logos, no UI, no watermark.`,

  // Toko Trip: the owner's words, lifted out of the first jetty brief and kept
  // here rather than in that one prompt — this is the register the whole game
  // is in, and the next Toko Trip asset should not have to restate it.
  //
  // Note what this block is doing that the others are not: it is describing a
  // MODEL SHEET, not a picture. Plain background, three-quarter view, no
  // texture detail, orthographic feel — every one of those is a photogrammetry
  // instruction. This style is built to be meshed, so the plate and the mesh
  // can share a spec instead of needing the cover/turnaround split the Neon
  // Ronin samurai needed.
  trip: `Toko Trip house style. Stylized LOW-POLY game asset in the manner of
A Short Hike and Monument Valley — simple faceted geometry, flat matte colours,
no texture detail, no grain, no noise. Soft ambient light with gentle flat
shading, no harsh shadows and no specular highlights. Three-quarter view with
an orthographic feel. Plain pure-white background, the object alone and whole
in frame with clear space around it. No text, no logos, no UI, no ground plane.`,

  // The lab: no game yet. A neutral character model sheet, for trying the
  // pipeline on something before it belongs to a cabinet. Deliberately has no
  // house colour in it — the moment one of these becomes a real character it
  // moves to that game's block and re-renders under its palette.
  lab: `Neutral character model sheet for 3D reconstruction. Full body, whole
figure in frame with clear space around it, nothing cropped. EVENLY lit from
the front with soft flat light — no dramatic shadows, no rim light, no cast
shadows, no atmosphere, no ground plane. Plain flat mid-grey background.
Stylized game-ready proportions, clean readable silhouette, matte surfaces with
no gloss and no fine texture detail. No text, no logos, no UI, no watermark.`,

  // Piritori → Eden and Toko Move: one style, two weathers. The hybrid the
  // owner locked on 2026-08-18 (ART_BIBLE.md §3.2) — ink-line illustration
  // carried on risograph print language, which is neither of the two things
  // it is made of and has to be described as the blend or a model picks one.
  // The night/day split is a palette line inside each prompt, never a second
  // style: the whole design claim is that these are one city.
  kallio: `Piritori house style. A hybrid: hard black ink-line illustration
with flat, slightly muted fills — Darkest Dungeon rather than a comic —
PRINTED as a risograph. Visible paper grain over everything, screen-print
colour separations, slight registration drift between colour layers. Limited
palette, hard seams between colours, flat cut-paper shapes where the
SILHOUETTE does the work rather than the shading. No gradients, no glow, no
lens effects, no photographic rendering. Helsinki in 2003 — cold, used,
deadpan, and not a crime film about it. No text, no logos, no UI, no
watermark. Include a small swatch strip of every colour used along the very
bottom edge, outside the subject.`,

  // Suds Jack: cold blooms, warm does not. The whole read of the game is that
  // what you want glows and what hurts you never does.
  suds: `Suds Jack house style. Everything desirable is COLD — pale cyan and
white soap bubbles, round, glowing, blooming with light. Everything hostile is
WARM — amber and rust grime, angular, matte, never glowing. Deep near-black
background. Long soft bloom on the cold elements only. No text, no UI.`,
};

export const DEFAULTS = {
  // "Nano banana" is Gemini 2.5 Flash Image. Newer image models exist in the
  // family (see scripts/lib/nano-banana.mjs) but the pinned default is the one
  // the owner named, and a model id is part of the hash — moving it restages
  // every 2D asset in the batch, which should be a decision and not a drift.
  imageModel: 'gemini-2.5-flash-image',
  // meshy-5 | meshy-6 | latest. Pinned to a REAL version on purpose: `latest`
  // resolves to whatever Meshy ships next, which would change the mesh without
  // changing the spec — and therefore without changing the hash the filename
  // is built from. A floating model id is precisely the drift this pipeline
  // exists to prevent, so the convenient value is the one we must not use.
  meshModel: 'meshy-6',
  aspect: '1:1',
  topology: 'triangle',
  targetPolycount: 12000,
};

